Media and lyric data
=====================

This repository does not contain, and does not distribute, any song audio or
full lyric text. Both are gitignored everywhere in this repository
(`data/audio/`, `data/clips/`, `data/references/`, `data/private/`,
`colab_handoff/colab_bundle.zip`, `colab_handoff/final_predictions/`) and are
never present in any tracked commit. What *is* tracked is metadata: YouTube
video IDs, LRCLIB reference IDs, derived counts (utterances, duration,
character-length statistics), and code. Reproducing the corpus locally
(`lyrics-asr-download-audio`, `lyrics-asr-fetch-lrclib`) fetches that media
directly from YouTube and LRCLIB onto the end user's own machine; nothing
copyrighted ever passes through this repository or its Git history.

**License check.** Every one of the 72 source videos in the corpus was
checked individually against YouTube's own license metadata
(`yt_dlp`, metadata-only, no video/audio downloaded for the check). The
result: all 72 are **Standard YouTube License** (All Rights Reserved — the
default YouTube license; i.e., standard copyright, not Creative Commons).
**Zero of the 72 are Creative Commons.** This is expected: the corpus is
commercial J-pop/J-rock, and none of it is released under a license that
would permit redistribution. Consequently this project treats every track as
fully copyrighted and never commits, bundles, or publishes the audio or
lyric text itself — only the identifiers needed to re-fetch it.

The "Owner (YouTube channel)" column below is the uploading YouTube channel
at the time of the check, not a legal determination of copyright ownership.
Most are the artist's or label's official channel; a few are third-party
uploads (fan channels, lyric-video channels) used because no official-channel
source existed within this project's duration-matching tolerance — see
`data/manifest.csv`'s `license` column and `config/benchmark_models.json`'s
corpus-gate rules for the exact selection policy. All 72 remain Standard
YouTube License regardless of uploader.

